<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gothenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Göteborg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gothenburg is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, on the west coast at the mouth of the Göta älv. Founded by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> in 1621, it gave Sweden an outlet to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> outside the Danish-controlled Sound. It became the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-east-india-company">Swedish East India Company</a> in 1731 and grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>'s largest port and an industrial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gothenburg.jpg" alt="Gothenburg" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bengt Nyman from Vaxholm, Sweden · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%B6teborg_2503_stitch_(28573994096).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gothenburg lies on the west coast of southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, about halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, at the mouth of the Göta älv, which feeds into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>, an arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Scandinavia's largest drainage basin enters the sea there, and the position has helped the city grow as a trading centre. The archipelago off the city consists of rough, barren rocks and cliffs, typical of the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohuslan">Bohuslän</a>. Because of the Gulf Stream the climate is mild, and warmer than that of places at a similar latitude such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early modern period the shape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s borders made Gothenburg strategically critical. It was the only Swedish gateway to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the Atlantic, on a very narrow strip of Swedish territory between Danish Halland in the south and Norwegian Bohuslän in the north. After several failed attempts, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> founded it by royal charter in 1621 as a heavily fortified, primarily <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, trading colony. An earlier city founded in 1604 had burned down in 1611.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> he gave generous privileges, such as tax relaxation, to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> allies, and he also attracted German and Scottish allies to populate his only town on the western coast. The city council of 1641 had four Swedish, three Dutch, three German and two Scottish members.</p><p>The charter of 1621 named the city Göteborg, after the Göta älv and other cities ending in -borg, and gave it at the same time the German and English name Gothenburg. Its trading status was furthered by the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-east-india-company">Swedish East India Company</a>. In the eighteenth century the Company made Gothenburg an important trade city, and imposing stone houses in Neo-Classical style were erected around the canals. In the nineteenth century the wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> moved outside the city walls, while the working class lived in wooden houses in the overcrowded district of Haga. Gothenburg became a city municipality with an elected council when the first Swedish local government acts were implemented in 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gothenburg is the second-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> and the seat of Västra Götaland County. Its port is the largest in the Nordic countries, and trade, shipping and manufacturing have long been the pillars of its economy. Because all but the military and royal houses were built of wood, very few buildings survive from the seventeenth century; among them are Kronhuset, the Torstenson Palace and the fortresses Skansen Kronan and Skansen Lejonet. The East India House now holds the Göteborg City Museum, and the city is home to the University of Gothenburg and Chalmers University of Technology.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gothenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Panels and animals are also in museums in Istanbul, Copenhagen, Detroit, <b>Gothenburg</b>, Munich, Vienna, Toronto, New York, Chicago and Boston, and in the Louvre.</blockquote><a href="/work/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1784 Louis XVI ceded the island to Sweden in exchange for trading rights in <b>Gothenburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-saint-barthelemy">French Saint-Barthélemy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1784 King Gustav III obtained Saint Barthélemy from France in exchange for French trading rights at <b>Gothenburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/saint-barthelemy-swedish">Saint Barthélemy (Swedish)</a></li></ul></section>
